1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,472
Week Three joins vascular access with an introduction to ECGs. The
school allocated module Seven G here, including conduction, systematic
rhythm checks, twelve-lead views and concerning ischemic patterns.

2
00:00:14,472 --> 00:00:28,680
This first lecture builds a repeatable rhythm method. The next lecture
teaches twelve-lead placement, the school three-minute exercise and
how recognition leads to communication rather than unsupported
independent treatment.

3
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:40,872
We use new synthetic teaching tracings with explicit scales. They
demonstrate features and measurements; they are not patient records, a
diagnostic simulator or the school assessment strips.

4
00:00:40,872 --> 00:00:53,376
Keep saying the school questions: what else could this be, what
changed after care, what should have changed and what got worse. Do
not let an interesting tracing displace immediate patient support.

5
00:00:53,376 --> 00:01:05,832
An ECG records electrical potential differences between skin
electrodes. It does not directly show blood pressure, stroke volume,
coronary anatomy or whether a palpable pulse exists.

6
00:01:05,832 --> 00:01:18,816
Assess responsiveness, breathing and circulation using the trained
primary assessment. Organized electrical complexes with no detectable
pulse can represent pulseless electrical activity.

7
00:01:18,816 --> 00:01:32,256
Conversely, a talking person with an apparent chaotic monitor trace
may have artifact. Assess the patient and equipment promptly without
assuming every unusual waveform is ventricular fibrillation.

8
00:01:32,256 --> 00:01:42,888
Use the monitor to add evidence to care. Never delay indicated
resuscitation to complete a six-step description or obtain the perfect
twelve-lead printout.

9
00:01:42,888 --> 00:01:54,432
In usual sinus activation, the sinoatrial node in the right atrium
initiates an impulse. Activation spreads through atrial tissue toward
the atrioventricular junction.

10
00:01:54,432 --> 00:02:06,960
The AV junction introduces a delay before activation passes into the
His bundle, bundle branches and Purkinje network. That sequence helps
coordinate filling and ventricular activation.

11
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:19,992
A delay or an alternative route can change timing and QRS appearance.
The strip is a surface consequence of electrical activity, not a
photograph of exactly where an abnormal impulse began.

12
00:02:19,992 --> 00:02:28,824
Trace the pathway aloud while looking at the figure, then identify
which measured event would change if atrioventricular conduction
slowed.

13
00:02:28,824 --> 00:02:39,576
Automaticity means some specialized cells can depolarize
spontaneously. A slower backup focus may emerge when faster impulses
no longer drive the system.

14
00:02:39,576 --> 00:02:50,400
The school gives typical adult teaching ranges of sixty to one hundred
for sinus activity, forty to sixty for junctional escape and twenty to
forty for ventricular escape.

15
00:02:50,400 --> 00:03:04,128
These ranges overlap with disease, medicines and physiological
variation. A rate alone does not identify a focus, and a backup
electrical rhythm may still fail to provide effective circulation.

16
00:03:04,128 --> 00:03:15,192
Assess the relation between rate and symptoms: new confusion, syncope,
chest discomfort or poor perfusion matters more than simply labelling
a number slow.

17
00:03:15,192 --> 00:03:29,304
Depolarization changes the electrical state of myocardial cells;
repolarization is their electrical recovery. These processes are
related to contraction and relaxation but are not identical to them.

18
00:03:29,304 --> 00:03:42,456
When a net electrical vector moves toward a positive viewing
electrode, the deflection often points upward; movement away can
produce a negative deflection. The same event can look different in
another lead.

19
00:03:42,456 --> 00:03:53,928
Do not interpret an upward waveform as good blood flow or a downward
waveform as a failed beat. Direction describes a view, not the
clinical success of the contraction.

20
00:03:53,928 --> 00:04:04,344
A lead is a calculated view, while an electrode is a physical sensor.
That distinction explains how ten electrodes can provide twelve views
in the next lecture.

21
00:04:04,344 --> 00:04:15,576
The P wave represents atrial depolarization. The QRS complex
represents ventricular depolarization, and the T wave represents
ventricular repolarization.

22
00:04:15,576 --> 00:04:28,920
A Q wave is an initial negative deflection before an R wave, R is a
positive deflection and S is a negative deflection after R. Not every
normal complex contains all three named components.

23
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:41,424
Identify the actual onset of the QRS, not simply the tall R peak, when
measuring PR, QRS or QT. An absent visible Q does not prevent
measuring those intervals.

24
00:04:41,424 --> 00:04:52,944
Use a clear baseline and a lead with visible boundaries. If motion or
overlapping waves obscure a boundary, state uncertainty rather than
inventing precision.

25
00:04:52,944 --> 00:05:04,128
At twenty-five millimetres per second, one millimetre horizontally
represents zero point zero four seconds, and five small boxes
represent zero point two seconds.

26
00:05:04,128 --> 00:05:14,544
At the usual gain of ten millimetres per millivolt, vertical height
represents voltage. Horizontal time and vertical voltage answer
different questions.

27
00:05:14,544 --> 00:05:24,816
A changed paper speed or gain changes the meaning of a box. Always
inspect the actual printout settings rather than applying a memorized
value to every display.

28
00:05:24,816 --> 00:05:36,552
Our six-second figures explicitly use the standard represented scale.
Browser zoom changes physical screen size, not the labelled time
relationships in the authored diagram.

29
00:05:36,552 --> 00:05:50,304
PR starts at the beginning of P and ends at the beginning of QRS. The
usual adult reference is zero point one two to zero point two zero
seconds, or three to five small boxes at standard speed.

30
00:05:50,304 --> 00:06:03,840
QRS width includes the whole ventricular depolarization complex, not
only the R wave. A width of zero point one two seconds or more is
classified as wide in this introductory rhythm method.

31
00:06:03,840 --> 00:06:15,960
The ST segment begins at the J point, where QRS ends. QT continues
from QRS onset through the end of T, including depolarization and
repolarization.

32
00:06:15,960 --> 00:06:29,304
The saved school table contains inconsistent decimal values such as a
zero point eight second P wave. Do not memorize those typographical
values; use the verified landmarks and measurements taught here.

33
00:06:29,304 --> 00:06:41,064
QT is not one fixed normal number for every heart rate and person.
Rate correction and the method used matter, as do difficult T-wave
boundaries and a wide QRS.

34
00:06:41,064 --> 00:06:54,240
A prolonged repolarization concern may relate to medications,
electrolyte disturbances or inherited conditions. A machine flag is a
reason for review, not a complete causal diagnosis.

35
00:06:54,240 --> 00:07:05,856
Do not calculate a correction from a poor tracing and treat its
apparent precision as certainty. Record the measured findings,
settings and symptoms and seek the appropriate support.

36
00:07:05,856 --> 00:07:18,216
The practical goal today is accurate acquisition and recognition of a
concern. Specialist risk calculation does not replace assessing
syncope, instability or a dangerous rhythm now.

37
00:07:18,216 --> 00:07:28,464
Explain the procedure, obtain appropriate consent and expose only what
is needed. Support the patient and limbs in a clinically tolerable
position.

38
00:07:28,464 --> 00:07:39,096
Prepare skin and electrode contact using the trained method. Sweat,
hair, poor adhesion, cold or a damaged cable can impair the signal.

39
00:07:39,096 --> 00:07:49,848
Verify the physical labels instead of assuming every manufacturer uses
the same colour code. Keep cable tension and unnecessary movement out
of the recording.

40
00:07:49,848 --> 00:07:59,976
Ask for a brief quiet recording with normal breathing when possible.
Check the result rather than assuming that a successful print means a
diagnostically useful signal.

41
00:07:59,976 --> 00:08:11,400
Movement, tremor, muscle activity, loose contact and electrical
interference can imitate disease. First compare the tracing with the
person's actual clinical state.

42
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:23,328
Look across available leads and for regular complexes continuing
through the noise. Check the cable and contact, reduce a reversible
noise source and reassess the signal.

43
00:08:23,328 --> 00:08:35,688
A comfortable talking patient and apparent VF strongly demand a signal
check. An unresponsive pulseless patient demands the arrest pathway
while appropriate equipment checks occur.

44
00:08:35,688 --> 00:08:47,712
Do not label every unexpected finding artifact. If it persists with a
good signal, describe it and obtain review; if the clinical condition
worsens, respond to that immediately.

45
00:08:47,712 --> 00:08:58,752
After confirming patient priority and signal quality, calculate rate
and assess regularity. Then inspect P waves and their relationship to
QRS complexes.

46
00:08:58,752 --> 00:09:08,664
Measure PR where it is measurable and measure the full QRS width.
Finish with a scan for concerning ST-T findings that may require a
twelve-lead view.

47
00:09:08,664 --> 00:09:19,080
Synthesize the observations rather than jumping to a label after the
first feature. A rate of one hundred and fifty does not alone
establish the rhythm origin.

48
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:29,760
State what you see, what you cannot assess and the clinical
consequence. This method supports recognition and escalation within
actual training and authorization.

49
00:09:29,760 --> 00:09:40,344
At standard speed, a regular rhythm with four large boxes between R
peaks has an estimated rate of three hundred divided by four, or
seventy-five per minute.

50
00:09:40,344 --> 00:09:52,392
For an irregular rhythm, count QRS complexes in a known six-second
interval and multiply by ten for an approximate average. Boundary and
short-sample effects limit precision.

51
00:09:52,392 --> 00:10:03,888
A monitor can double-count tall T waves or miss low complexes. Compare
its rate with your tracing calculation and clinical pulse rather than
copying the display uncritically.

52
00:10:03,888 --> 00:10:13,992
In atrial-ventricular dissociation, atrial and ventricular rates can
differ. For perfusion-related reasoning, make clear which rate you are
reporting.

53
00:10:13,992 --> 00:10:23,064
Compare successive R-to-R intervals across enough of the strip.
Calipers or a paper edge can make a subtle pattern easier to see.

54
00:10:23,064 --> 00:10:32,856
A repeating irregular pattern differs from a rhythm with no repeating
interval sequence. That description helps the receiving clinician
understand the evidence.

55
00:10:32,856 --> 00:10:43,320
Check whether apparent variation comes from motion, missed complexes
or a very short sample. A longer strip or another lead may clarify the
pattern.

56
00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:54,840
Regularity alone does not determine safety. A regular rhythm can be
dangerously fast or slow, while an irregular rhythm can still have a
pulse and require non-arrest assessment.

57
00:10:54,840 --> 00:11:21,432
The assigned nursing video offers a useful six-step rhythm method, but
a shortcut is only valid under its assumptions. In this original ten-
second example, twelve identifiable ventricular complexes give an
estimated rate of seventy-two per minute. Do not multiply by ten
unless the measured strip really spans six seconds. Check speed,
signal and actual duration before defending a number.

58
00:11:21,432 --> 00:11:47,328
Then explain the patient, not only the strip. Assess perfusion and
symptoms, describe P waves and their relationship to the ventricular
complexes, and measure the relevant intervals. A normal-looking rate
does not prove useful circulation. This rhythm sequence must not
exclude ST or QT assessment when the clinical problem requires them,
and it does not replace the separate twelve-lead acquisition lesson.

59
00:11:47,328 --> 00:11:58,992
In a usual sinus pattern, similar P waves precede each QRS and have an
appropriate relationship to ventricular activation. Lead orientation
changes their appearance.

60
00:11:58,992 --> 00:12:11,112
Look for extra atrial activity, P waves not followed by QRS or QRS
complexes without a clearly preceding P. Count relationships rather
than assuming one bump means normal.

61
00:12:11,112 --> 00:12:24,264
At fast rates or with low amplitude, a P may be buried in another
wave. The school's simple missing-P rule cannot prove that sinus
origin is excluded in every noisy or fast tracing.

62
00:12:24,264 --> 00:12:34,728
If a P cannot be identified reliably, report that it is not clearly
visible. Seek a better lead or review rather than inventing a PR
interval.

63
00:12:34,728 --> 00:12:46,776
Measure from the first P deflection to the first QRS deflection. A
prolonged PR indicates delayed atrioventricular conduction but does
not by itself precisely locate the delay.

64
00:12:46,776 --> 00:12:57,192
Examine more than one beat. A changing interval, extra P waves or
dropped QRS complexes provides different evidence from a consistently
long interval.

65
00:12:57,192 --> 00:13:09,576
A short PR can have several explanations. Do not diagnose a specific
accessory pathway solely from one short interval without the other
features and appropriate expertise.

66
00:13:09,576 --> 00:13:22,032
For today, describe the measured relationship and patient condition.
Symptomatic poor perfusion or a concerning block pattern needs prompt
escalation rather than a long naming exercise.

67
00:13:22,032 --> 00:13:33,024
Measure the complete QRS from onset to end in a lead with clear
boundaries. At standard speed, three small boxes represent zero point
one two seconds.

68
00:13:33,024 --> 00:13:43,056
A narrow complex commonly reflects activation through a relatively
rapid ventricular conduction route. It does not prove that every
upstream process is normal.

69
00:13:43,056 --> 00:13:55,560
A wide complex can arise from ventricular origin, bundle-branch
conduction delay, pacing or other effects. Do not use wide equals
ventricular as a universal diagnostic equation.

70
00:13:55,560 --> 00:14:08,496
A rapid wide-complex pattern is potentially dangerous. Assess pulse
and instability, obtain appropriate support and use the actual
authorized pathway rather than guessing a drug from width.

71
00:14:08,496 --> 00:14:21,024
Identify the baseline and J point before describing ST displacement.
Noise, QRS conduction abnormalities and the selected lead can make a
quick single-strip judgment unreliable.

72
00:14:21,024 --> 00:14:33,696
Notice clear elevation, depression or concerning T-wave changes and
obtain the appropriate twelve-lead view and review. The next lecture
teaches lead groupings and the qualified criteria.

73
00:14:33,696 --> 00:14:46,032
Do not apply the school shortcut of two millimetres in every chest
lead. Criteria depend on the actual lead and, particularly for V two
and V three, patient category.

74
00:14:46,032 --> 00:14:57,576
A clean normal strip does not rule out coronary ischemia or another
dangerous cause of symptoms. Continue assessment, repeat when
indicated and communicate the full clinical picture.

75
00:14:57,576 --> 00:15:08,184
Figure A has six QRS complexes over six seconds and a one-second
R-to-R interval. Both methods give sixty per minute in this teaching
strip.

76
00:15:08,184 --> 00:15:18,168
Similar visible P waves precede each narrow QRS with a consistent
relationship. That supports a usual sinus description in this
schematic.

77
00:15:18,168 --> 00:15:30,264
The same person could still have coronary ischemia, bleeding, sepsis
or another problem. A baseline rhythm is not proof of stable
physiology or absence of a treatment indication.

78
00:15:30,264 --> 00:15:37,896
Say the rhythm findings, then say how the patient is doing. Those are
related but separate parts of a meaningful report.

79
00:15:37,896 --> 00:15:48,048
The slow teaching strip has a one-and-a-half-second R-to-R interval,
corresponding to forty per minute. Now add the patient information
before judging urgency.

80
00:15:48,048 --> 00:15:58,968
A well person with a known low baseline differs from someone with new
faintness, chest pressure and poor perfusion. Do not treat the number
without the clinical consequence.

81
00:15:58,968 --> 00:16:12,480
Review oxygenation, ventilation, circulation, medication effects and
other plausible causes while providing necessary support. A sinus
label does not make symptomatic bradycardia harmless.

82
00:16:12,480 --> 00:16:26,280
Report the onset, symptoms, pulse, blood pressure trend and ECG
relationship. This lesson does not authorize independent pacing or an
advanced drug merely because the rate is low.

83
00:16:26,280 --> 00:16:37,752
A rapid rate may compensate for another problem or arise from a
primary rhythm disturbance. Treating every fast number as an isolated
rhythm problem risks missing the cause.

84
00:16:37,752 --> 00:16:48,720
Describe whether it is regular, whether P waves can be assessed and
whether QRS is narrow or wide. Then integrate onset, symptoms and
perfusion.

85
00:16:48,720 --> 00:17:00,936
Consider plausible causes such as pain, fever, volume loss, medication
or substance effects, and do not dismiss a sick patient as anxious
without appropriate assessment.

86
00:17:00,936 --> 00:17:12,000
Provide indicated supportive care and obtain the appropriate clinical
review. A memorized label is not authorization for an antiarrhythmic
or a shock in a patient with a pulse.

87
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:22,224
This new schematic has unequal intervals and no clearly consistent P
waves. Those are the observed features, not a complete patient
diagnosis.

88
00:17:22,224 --> 00:17:32,208
An irregularly irregular ventricular response without consistent P
waves can suggest atrial fibrillation, but a short or noisy sample can
mislead.

89
00:17:32,208 --> 00:17:41,592
Seek a useful longer strip or other lead when appropriate, check
whether the pulse and monitor agree and assess symptoms, rate and
perfusion.

90
00:17:41,592 --> 00:17:53,640
Do not infer the duration of the rhythm, anticoagulation need or a
safe discharge decision from this introductory pattern. Communicate
findings and obtain the actual authorized review.

91
00:17:53,640 --> 00:18:05,928
A ventricular tachycardia pattern commonly appears as rapid wide
complexes. It can occur with a pulse or without one; that distinction
cannot be made by the tracing alone.

92
00:18:05,928 --> 00:18:18,360
Pulseless VT and ventricular fibrillation are shockable arrest
rhythms. Follow the trained arrest and AED or authorized defibrillator
sequence while maintaining high-quality resuscitation.

93
00:18:18,360 --> 00:18:28,512
VF lacks organized, identifiable QRS complexes. Check the clinical
state, because movement artifact can resemble it in a perfusing
patient.

94
00:18:28,512 --> 00:18:39,864
Do not give an unsynchronized arrest shock to a conscious perfusing
patient merely because the screen is wide and fast. Obtain immediate
appropriate support for instability.

95
00:18:39,864 --> 00:18:54,336
An apparent flatline may be asystole, but disconnected leads, the
selected view or equipment settings can create a misleading display.
Verify appropriately without prolonging interruptions in necessary
resuscitation.

96
00:18:54,336 --> 00:19:06,360
Ventricular asystole means absent detectable ventricular activity and
can occasionally coexist with atrial activity. It is not made
shockable by increasing monitor gain.

97
00:19:06,360 --> 00:19:18,552
PEA is a clinical combination of organized electrical activity and no
detectable pulse. It can look like an ordinary rhythm on paper;
assessment of circulation is essential.

98
00:19:18,552 --> 00:19:31,272
Both require the actual non-shockable arrest pathway and attention to
reversible causes. Complete resuscitation team procedures are
developed further in the school cardiac-resuscitation weekend.

99
00:19:31,272 --> 00:19:41,472
In this original example, four large boxes between R peaks gives
seventy-five per minute. Consistent spacing means the sampled rhythm
is regular.

100
00:19:41,472 --> 00:19:51,936
Four small boxes for PR gives zero point one six seconds. Two small
boxes for QRS gives zero point zero eight seconds, a narrow complex.

101
00:19:51,936 --> 00:20:05,640
Similar P waves preceding each QRS with that relationship supports a
sinus description. It does not answer whether the patient's chest
pressure is coronary, vascular, pulmonary or another problem.

102
00:20:05,640 --> 00:20:17,160
Continue the primary assessment, obtain the appropriate twelve-lead
and consider alternatives while care proceeds. A regular rhythm at
seventy-five is not a clearance test.

103
00:20:17,160 --> 00:20:28,320
Without reading the slide, trace the conduction pathway and name the
electrical meaning of P, QRS and T. Explain why none independently
proves a pulse.

104
00:20:28,320 --> 00:20:39,912
Calculate the rate for three, four and five large boxes between
regular R peaks, then convert four small boxes to seconds. State which
settings must be verified first.

105
00:20:39,912 --> 00:20:53,424
Compare rapid wide-complex activity with and without a pulse, then
organized activity with and without a pulse. Explain the change in
urgency and pathway without inventing new permissions.

106
00:20:53,424 --> 00:21:05,784
Take the same disciplined observation into the next lesson: ten
correctly placed electrodes, a readable twelve-lead, rapid
communication and reassessment after every intervention.
